 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "freezer keeps beeping"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "A beeping fridge-freezer is usually a safety alarm telling you either the door isn't sealed properly or the temperature inside has risen too high. First, please check that both doors are fully closed and nothing (like a food packet) is blocking them from sealing tight. Then, touch the food in the freezer: if it is still rock hard frozen, it’s likely just a door-ajar alarm; if it feels soft or thawed, the appliance may be failing to cool and needs attention sooner."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "the bottom freezer drawer wasn't pushed in properly last night, a bag was sticking out. ice cream is a bit soft round the edges but everything else is hard"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "That explains the beep perfectly. The drawer being open let warm air in, which raised the temperature just enough to trigger the alarm and soften the ice cream at the edges; since everything else is still hard, it hasn't been a cooling failure. Please push the drawer fully back until it clicks or seats firmly, ensuring no bags are sticking out. The beeping should stop within a few minutes once the sensor detects the door is closed. If it continues to beep after 15–20 minutes with the drawer shut and cold, let me know—we can then check if the temperature has dropped sufficiently."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "ok pushed it shut. will wait and see. the temp number is still flashing -9 though"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Since the ice cream was only slightly soft and everything else is hard, this is likely just the system catching up after the warm air entered. Leave it closed for 30–60 minutes; if the flashing stops and shows a steady number (like -18), it’s working normally. If it stays flashing or beeping after an hour with the drawer shut, let me know."
  }
 ],
